===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To fall as a [[waterfall]] or series of small waterfalls.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 August 26|title=Network News: Major flood damage severs key Edinburgh-Glasgow rail artery|journal=Rail|page=21|text=Services between Glasgow Queen Street and Edinburgh Waverley via Falkirk High are currently suspended, following a 30-metre breach of the Union Canal that occurred on August 12 after torrential rain and thunderstorms. The thousands of gallons of water that '''cascaded''' onto the railway line below washed away track, ballast and overhead line equipment, and undermined embankments along a 300-metre section of Scotland's busiest rail link.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To arrange in a stepped series like a [[waterfall]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Greg M Perry|title=Sams teach yourself Microsoft Windows XP in 24 hours|passage=No matter how you tile or '''cascade''' the windows, each window's Minimize, Maximize, and Restore buttons work as usual.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To occur as a causal [[sequence]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=Adam Freeman; Allen Jones|title=Programming .NET Security
|passage=Child folders inherit the configuration of their parent folder, meaning that configuration settings '''cascade''' down through an application's virtual folder hierarchy.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Rhodri Clark|title=TfW seeks PRM derogation for Class 37 sets|journal=Modern Railways|page=87|text=Relief arrived at Cardiff Canton depot on 1 September in the shape of the first of 12 Class 170 units '''cascaded''' from Greater Anglia.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|slang}} To [[vomit]].<ref>{{R:1913}}</ref>

